Here are the most common methods to center a div horizontally in CSS, depending on your layout needs.

### 1. The Modern Standard: Flexbox
This is currently the most popular and robust method for aligning items within a container. It works well even if you don't know the width of the child element.

**HTML:**
```html
<div class="container">
    <div class="child"></div>
</div>
```

**CSS:**
```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center; /* Centers children along the main axis (horizontal) */
}
```

---

### 2. For Block-Level Elements with Known Widths: Margins
If your div has a specific fixed or percentage width, using `margin: auto` is the classic approach. The browser automatically calculates equal space on both sides.

**CSS:**
```css
.child {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;

    /* Shorthand version */
    margin: 0 auto; 

    /* You must define a width for this to work effectively */
    width: 50%; 
}
```

---

### 3. Using CSS Grid (The "One-Liner")
Similar to Flexbox but often easier if you are already working with grid layouts. It centers content by default in many scenarios, or can be explicitly set.

**HTML:**
```html
<div class="container">
    <div class="child"></div>
</div>
```

**CSS:**
```css
.container {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center; /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */

    /* Or specifically horizontal only using justify-self on the child */
}
.child {
    justify-self: center; 
}
```

---

### 4. Absolute Positioning (For Floating Elements)
Useful if you want to position an element relative to its parent without affecting other elements in the flow.

**CSS:**
```css
.parent {
    position: relative; /* Establishes a positioning context */
}

.child {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;          /* Moves the left edge of the div to the middle of the parent */
    transform: translateX(-50%); /* Shifts it back by half its own width */
}
```
